# Target C-II — every diameter direction is an exact hull normal, so birth order is direction order

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only geometric sharpening; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** all actual diameter edges; high-`t` consequences stated for the selected O--O path  
**Dependencies:** convexity of the full source hull; canonical C67 separation; selected-path host-arc topology  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_exact_diameter_normal_order_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Several current high-`t` arguments compare an actual diameter chord with the radial direction or a hull support normal using Type-II near-disk errors. For a true diameter edge this approximation is unnecessary.

If `xy` is an actual diameter edge of the convex source hull `K`, then the chord vector itself is an **exact support normal**:

\[
\boxed{
x-y\in N_K(x),
\qquad
y-x\in N_K(y).
}
\tag{DN.1}

Equivalently, the two endpoint outward normals selected by the diameter are exactly antipodal, and the projective diameter direction is exactly one of the support-normal directions at either endpoint.

This gives two useful high-`t` consequences.

1. The host-forced transverse-margin theorem needs no radial/midpoint approximation: a switching diameter born on one of the two physical host arcs inherits the host support-normal interval **exactly**. Its fixed `arcsin(0.4143)` transverse margin loses only the existing `o(1)` row-phase/terminal-energy error.
2. On a fixed birth host, distinct switching diameters born in ordered layer subarcs have strictly ordered diameter directions. The order is the convex support-normal order itself, not merely an approximate near-disk order. At the partner endpoint the outward normal is exactly the antipode of the birth normal, so the same direction parameter is transported exactly to the destination boundary.

Thus every fixed-host nested rail in the current frontier is simultaneously an endpoint-ordered and an exact normal-direction-ordered rail.

---

## 1. Diameter endpoints are exact support points in the chord direction

Let `K` be any compact convex set of diameter `D`, and let `x,y in K` satisfy

\[
|x-y|=D.
\]

Take arbitrary `z in K`. Since `D` is the diameter,

\[
|z-y|\le D=|x-y|.
\]

Expand around `x`:

\[
|z-y|^2
=|x-y|^2+2(x-y)\cdot(z-x)+|z-x|^2.
\]

Therefore

\[
2(x-y)\cdot(z-x)+|z-x|^2\le0,
\]

so in particular

\[
\boxed{
(x-y)\cdot(z-x)\le0
\quad\text{for every }z\in K.
}
\tag{DN.2}

This is exactly the support-normal condition

\[
x-y\in N_K(x).
\]

Interchanging `x,y` gives

\[
y-x\in N_K(y).
\]

This proves (DN.1). Convexity is only needed to use the usual normal-cone language; the inequality itself follows directly from the diameter property.

A useful width reformulation is

\[
\boxed{
w_K(d)=D}
\tag{DN.3}

for `d=(x-y)/D`, with `x` and `y` lying on the two supporting lines orthogonal to `d` at distance `D`.

---

## 2. Exact host transverse margin

Let the common selected row phase be `[u_*]`. The coupled-path terminal-energy argument gives, on either physical host arc, an oriented support-normal interval centered at one of the branches `+Ju_*` or `-Ju_*` with half-width

\[
\vartheta_*+o(1),
\qquad
\vartheta_*:=\arccos(0.4143)<{\pi\over2}.
\tag{DN.4}

Take any switching diameter `xy` at a nonhub selected interface and let `x` be its unique birth endpoint in the layer. The path-region switching identity places `x` on one of the two physical host subarcs. Orient the chord outward at the birth endpoint:

\[
n_h:={x-y\over D}.
\tag{DN.5}

By (DN.1), `n_h` itself is an outward support normal at `x`. Hence directly from (DN.4),

\[
\angle(n_h,\sigma Ju_*)
\le\vartheta_*+o(1)
\tag{DN.6}

for the appropriate host sign `sigma`.

Since `[u_i]=[u_*]+o(1)` for every selected row,

\[
\boxed{
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([n_h],[u_i])
\ge {\pi\over2}-\vartheta_*-o(1)
=\arcsin(0.4143)-o(1).}
\tag{DN.7}

Thus the fixed transverse margin is an exact convex-diameter consequence once the host normal interval is known. No circumcenter, radial approximation, or midpoint estimate is required.

---

## 3. Birth order is exact diameter-direction order

Fix one physical host arc and orient it along the ambient convex boundary so that outward support-normal angle is nondecreasing. Let

\[
I_1<I_2<\cdots<I_M
\tag{DN.8}

be pairwise interior-disjoint layer subarcs in this boundary order. For each `s`, let `x_s in I_s` be the birth endpoint of an actual diameter edge `x_s y_s`, and orient its diameter normal outward at birth:

\[
n_s={x_s-y_s\over D}.
\tag{DN.9}

By (DN.1), `n_s in N_K(x_s)`. Normal cones of a convex boundary are ordered: if `x_s` precedes `x_t`, then any lifted normal angle selected from the interior of `N_K(x_s)` cannot exceed any lifted normal angle selected from the interior of `N_K(x_t)`, except for a possible shared boundary normal at adjacent vertices.

The current source has canonical C67 separation for distinct actual diameter edges:

\[
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}([n_s],[n_t])
\ge2\arcsin{1\over2D}>0.
\tag{DN.10}

Hence distinct selected diameter edges cannot use the same normal line. On any host normal interval of width less than `pi`, the projective line has a unique compatible oriented lift. Therefore the weak convex normal order is strict:

\[
\boxed{
\arg n_1<\arg n_2<\cdots<\arg n_M
}
\tag{DN.11}

up to one global reversal of the chosen host orientation.

So an ordered family of birth layers already carries an exact ordered family of actual diameter directions.

---

## 4. The partner endpoint transports the same normal parameter exactly

At the partner endpoint `y_s`, (DN.1) gives the outward normal

\[
{y_s-x_s\over D}=-n_s.
\tag{DN.12}

Thus if `alpha_s` is a lifted outward-normal angle at the birth endpoint, then the diameter selects the partner normal

\[
\boxed{\alpha_s+\pi}
\tag{DN.13}

exactly, not merely up to `o(1)`.

Consequently every diameter rail transports one scalar normal parameter from the birth boundary to the destination boundary by the antipodal map. In particular:

- an opposite-host rail matches a strictly ordered birth-normal sequence to its exact antipodal sequence on the opposite host;
- a far-terminal rail forces the terminal boundary piece to contain the exact antipodes of the birth-normal sequence;
- a one-death-interface plateau likewise forces that single destination piece to carry the full antipodal normal span of the selected birth family.

Using C67, any `M` distinct bars on one such lifted normal branch have span at least

\[
\boxed{
(M-1)\,2\arcsin{1\over2D}.
}
\tag{DN.14}

For `M=lambda D-o(D)`, the destination support-normal span is therefore at least

\[
\boxed{\lambda-o(1)}
\tag{DN.15}

radians. This recovers the terminal macroscopic-span phenomenon directly in normal coordinates and applies equally to any fixed physical destination piece.

---

## 5. Same-host diameter exclusion becomes immediate

The selected physical host arc has support-normal width at most

\[
2\vartheta_*+o(1)<\pi.
\tag{DN.16}

If a diameter had both endpoints on that same host arc, (DN.1) would require the host normal set to contain two antipodal normals whose oriented difference is exactly `pi`. This contradicts (DN.16).

Thus the existing same-host diameter exclusion follows directly from exact diameter normality and the terminal-energy host-width bound. The earlier chord-length computation remains valid, but the normal proof is shorter and exposes the order structure needed later.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The current high-`t` rail should now be viewed in normal coordinates:

```text
ordered birth layer
-> exact outward diameter normal at the birth vertex
-> strict normal-direction order along the host
-> exact antipodal normal at the partner vertex
-> nested support/death order on the destination boundary.
```

This removes another approximation layer from the remaining geometric problem. The straight diameter direction is not merely close to the boundary normal because the source is near circular; it **is** a boundary support normal exactly.

The unresolved step is to combine this exact antipodal normal transport with the selected-train contact/terminal normal sequences. In the opposite-host branch one must show that the required normal matching is incompatible with the ordered near-triangular train grid or KKT force orientation; in the far-terminal branch one must overcharge the terminal normal span or terminal ledger.

No such final incompatibility is claimed here.

**Disposition:** proved exact support-normality of every diameter edge, upgraded fixed-host rails to exact normal-ordered rails, and transported their direction parameter exactly to the partner boundary. C-II and Level I remain open.